#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105615 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59392c4-996f-5b29-9c37-7a1b75ffd896" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vector for a Positively Oriented Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "4b8266ba-b293-541f-9e4f-5005a6184816" :question/math-academy-id "e-9566" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $x^2+y^2 = 2.$ Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C.$" :question/worked-solution "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14052255/assets/0c0a4b177a997ffb38489abc902a1c78d027d0da661b1498fc97d3d9a77862d9.png)\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector $\\mathbf r'(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14052255/assets/0cd188ffe544d454a3375df5f043096888f6dfd5243551d35502efda4bcfeb11.png)\n\nRotating the vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ clockwise, we get\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14052255/assets/5f0d414a5388ae374f46f75825b9ffec7725649a97a45de24f8178d95a94b1ad.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||\\mathbf r'(t)|| = \\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{2}\\sin t)^2 + (\\sqrt{2}\\cos t)^2} = \\sqrt{2},\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t) = \\sqrt{2}\\cos{t}$ and $y(t) = \\sqrt{2}\\sin{t},$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105616 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad522514-328e-5944-b4c7-91d7e3b302f0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vector for a Negatively Oriented Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "ad8da3b7-7e57-58bb-a0d2-8cdbddf80239" :question/math-academy-id "e-9571" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $\\mathbf r(t) =  12\\sin t\\,\\mathbf{i} + 9\\cos t \\,\\mathbf{j}$ for $t \\in [0,2\\pi).$ Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C.$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $\\mathbf r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14052255/assets/007f19fb0c6e24115d719178a4b44e78c9881b9f71f52d0f2970480358e3faab.png)\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector $\\mathbf r'(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a $90^\\circ$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14052255/assets/ec0ddbbff34ed096bb872f23663f97005932a4ea8f368059cc4b511a5c9c76cc.png)\n\nRotating the vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14052255/assets/6f74bda044d15ed504a0b95b1e60ae8542f2b304f1e2712ae8a1d840a45aeaad.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{\\sqrt{144{cos}^{2}⁡t+81{sin}^{2}⁡t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105966 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad522514-328e-5944-b4c7-91d7e3b302f0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vector for a Negatively Oriented Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b10faf1c-90b3-5a7c-a578-3d04dcc5bdaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\cos t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102044} {:answer/id #uuid "330860e8-f7c6-516a-9777-3e8301627fab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\sin t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102045} {:answer/id #uuid "511b7773-28b6-5728-9cc7-095802b34ba2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\sin t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102046} {:answer/id #uuid "58ce2e71-d841-5ebb-882a-fc2fbdc26b2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7\\cos t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102047} {:answer/id #uuid "75ad3d7b-8d9e-567e-91b3-ff03d3eeda41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24\\cos t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102048}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b10faf1c-90b3-5a7c-a578-3d04dcc5bdaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\cos t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102044} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd1c69f7-0887-5669-9c72-f0a7a3dc56cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102043}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "62471caf-151d-5553-a12b-9dfaa035554e" :question/math-academy-id "q-126826" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+24\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $b(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 24\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -24\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -24\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}24\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step $2.$\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}24\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{24\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $b(t)=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105967 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59392c4-996f-5b29-9c37-7a1b75ffd896" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vector for a Positively Oriented Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2789748-04c4-50cd-8827-f539105a2edc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3x \\\\ 3y\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329639} {:answer/id #uuid "f19f71b8-d8c0-57e5-9444-73d6abe4cd11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329640} {:answer/id #uuid "566591da-6b96-581e-9ec6-c1c418c1e0db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329641} {:answer/id #uuid "cb20230f-a2f4-5a79-a333-b9f89a3ddfca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}y \\\\ -x\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329642} {:answer/id #uuid "4db618af-8a69-5e48-94fb-801b12557084" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329643}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4db618af-8a69-5e48-94fb-801b12557084" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329643} :answer-field/id #uuid "0dcca08d-368c-56de-8a85-69182c2ff363" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329638}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6ad4fbe5-37f0-5999-9ae5-70bbcca8a444" :question/math-academy-id "q-126830" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$ Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C.$" :question/worked-solution "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi )\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||{r}^{′}(t)||=\\sqrt{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}=3,\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329644 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59392c4-996f-5b29-9c37-7a1b75ffd896" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vector for a Positively Oriented Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ed5e618-8149-5ce3-a8f9-82e79fda80d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329646} {:answer/id #uuid "b808ca10-9dc1-5240-8ce4-79fc6a7d5530" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}y \\\\ -x\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329647} {:answer/id #uuid "e36e32bd-2e3b-5081-8a8a-8195953bea76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2x \\\\ 2y\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329648} {:answer/id #uuid "8859cc49-7d10-5c69-93d9-c1f9966fc9e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329649} {:answer/id #uuid "6a691c9f-0008-5369-9d38-d87f921d1499" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329650}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ed5e618-8149-5ce3-a8f9-82e79fda80d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329646} :answer-field/id #uuid "074da1c0-2d18-5c65-9b7b-cd11dc0df404" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329645}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0a627b8d-c420-5787-824a-d90fe7d71973" :question/math-academy-id "q-126917" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C.$" :question/worked-solution "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi )\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||{r}^{′}(t)||=\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}=2,\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y(t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329651 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59392c4-996f-5b29-9c37-7a1b75ffd896" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vector for a Positively Oriented Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9f10c99-5684-5c84-bf9e-57755c76d25b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5x \\\\ 5y\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329653} {:answer/id #uuid "fa0f037b-666d-5233-9c87-78d9bf5a5211" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329654} {:answer/id #uuid "290b6357-377e-50ef-a29c-9b84c6704c9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329655} {:answer/id #uuid "22b96cb5-f864-5042-8620-dd53a3d4132f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}y \\\\ -x\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329656} {:answer/id #uuid "dae2d0c6-552f-5cde-a92d-038dc290c8ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa0f037b-666d-5233-9c87-78d9bf5a5211" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329654} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fc5ab1c-f446-5d65-8b58-d2939ecb248c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329652}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a1cd122-4bd6-53f7-ba19-ee5b28cab729" :question/math-academy-id "q-126920" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25.$ Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C.$" :question/worked-solution "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi )\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||{r}^{′}(t)||=\\sqrt{(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}=5,\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y(t)=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329658 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59392c4-996f-5b29-9c37-7a1b75ffd896" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vector for a Positively Oriented Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9a8512c-fa58-5b23-a65d-9c6df192e451" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329660} {:answer/id #uuid "5b81d5c0-2675-54f3-9e56-b3dad84809b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}y \\\\ -x\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329661} {:answer/id #uuid "3685642e-7098-529a-8bf8-d80626c7acfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329662} {:answer/id #uuid "244b6d27-19f9-50bb-88a2-42b0e055166f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329663} {:answer/id #uuid "f825a33e-bacd-52c1-a2f7-9a94652c8f2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}x \\\\ \\sqrt{5}y\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329664}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9a8512c-fa58-5b23-a65d-9c6df192e451" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329660} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1c67856-58f7-5947-aa91-d81c17702333" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329659}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6ae0c8fb-626b-544b-9719-70c11c43a0cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-126922" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$ Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C.$" :question/worked-solution "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi )\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||{r}^{′}(t)||=\\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}=\\sqrt{5},\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t)=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y(t)=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329665 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad522514-328e-5944-b4c7-91d7e3b302f0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vector for a Negatively Oriented Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f31a898b-ccbf-5c80-9b36-cf5887b16ea8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329667} {:answer/id #uuid "c0a6ac45-3318-598d-a5ab-fad1ff2863c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329668} {:answer/id #uuid "1d0bfe9d-3939-59ae-b9be-6af084c625a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329669} {:answer/id #uuid "9362173a-7d61-5d05-a272-3f68f2844890" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329670} {:answer/id #uuid "0592b4d2-c460-5765-9941-58fdaa471987" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329671}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f31a898b-ccbf-5c80-9b36-cf5887b16ea8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329667} :answer-field/id #uuid "12598669-8b2a-54e9-8c78-b3f07c2226e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329666}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6fd9957a-66b4-5ce3-b176-b797a4c5cba6" :question/math-academy-id "q-126927" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $a(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329672 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad522514-328e-5944-b4c7-91d7e3b302f0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vector for a Negatively Oriented Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1cf0c75-c19a-5f0e-a043-feffba6c0927" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329674} {:answer/id #uuid "89c0d3e2-b793-5475-92fa-2108163b88aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329675} {:answer/id #uuid "0bd6c253-3d91-5ba3-872e-af8f561b25a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329676} {:answer/id #uuid "b348b303-6e2b-5ff8-8502-a5d50019517a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329677} {:answer/id #uuid "8bef1330-eaae-5e48-8bdd-2e6d57afebfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329678}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1cf0c75-c19a-5f0e-a043-feffba6c0927" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329674} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa0e98dd-673f-585b-90af-48c0fd69e301" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d4fb3d46-e8f4-590c-bf7e-7af7a18d54d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-126932" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $a(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329679 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59392c4-996f-5b29-9c37-7a1b75ffd896" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vector for a Positively Oriented Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef05a31f-2d86-5647-8f48-657ffa8c2d1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}y \\\\ -x\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329681} {:answer/id #uuid "72cf8cb7-93e6-5fb8-b9e8-7201eb6db2fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}[\\begin{aligned}-x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329682} {:answer/id #uuid "0199b376-aa3e-51f5-bc1c-1cf2f7ae0b4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329683} {:answer/id #uuid "219cfa02-806a-5ff8-9f58-ee8ea3a18534" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329684} {:answer/id #uuid "2df6c35b-fc38-5618-9a1d-57a449e35424" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329685}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "219cfa02-806a-5ff8-9f58-ee8ea3a18534" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329684} :answer-field/id #uuid "d944666f-31c2-5c68-853c-7a17089c5cbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329680}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c996f89f-de6d-5978-856b-5758e6e8d01b" :question/math-academy-id "q-149682" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=7.$ Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C.$" :question/worked-solution "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi )\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||{r}^{′}(t)||=\\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}=\\sqrt{7},\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t)=\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y(t)=\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329686 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad522514-328e-5944-b4c7-91d7e3b302f0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vector for a Negatively Oriented Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56ac0d39-6aff-5d6f-955c-8c305dd626a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329688} {:answer/id #uuid "3376f7da-115f-5182-bedc-b679b49acf31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329689} {:answer/id #uuid "332ed1e5-afa8-5f7e-ade8-7bfbd4ef7934" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329690} {:answer/id #uuid "9ba30f0f-f3dc-533e-bfe2-e2c5e4978ed6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329691} {:answer/id #uuid "32a227c4-b81c-5a78-bb23-721e64c3514f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329692}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56ac0d39-6aff-5d6f-955c-8c305dd626a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329688} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cf58bc9-51b6-5940-9a44-e4f441652668" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329687}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bba80616-72c9-5066-a9a1-cb9a83607d7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-149685" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $a(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329693 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad522514-328e-5944-b4c7-91d7e3b302f0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vector for a Negatively Oriented Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16ac3094-3922-53b5-b668-013aa53c5fba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329695} {:answer/id #uuid "87e7395f-e4c7-5a13-ac5b-a6d16c5396fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329696} {:answer/id #uuid "944cd1d5-0d61-5a86-a25d-4f99cc96a6a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329697} {:answer/id #uuid "5457778c-9925-5d84-994b-36bec54afb09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329698} {:answer/id #uuid "d49ebca8-074c-543e-86a5-9130632eb6e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329699}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16ac3094-3922-53b5-b668-013aa53c5fba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329695} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c618bf3-5a2d-50d0-86e1-a668c8bc098c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329694}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "095fb5b0-13ee-5063-92ab-6c3183cdb095" :question/math-academy-id "q-149688" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $b(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $b(t)=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$"}]}
